    "prefix"
  ],
  "text": "Every token a model reads, writes or thinks ends up on a bill. Your prompt is what it reads. The answer is what it writes. And a reasoning model's thinking is written too, even though you never see it. So what does a token actually cost?\n\nEvery big provider prices tokens the same way: five numbers, each per million tokens. Input is the baseline. Output, five times that or more. Cached input, a tenth or less. Writing to the cache, a little over input. And batch, about half off. The prices keep falling. The ratios hardly move.\n\nSo why does output cost so much more? It's not a pricing whim. It's the computation. Your prompt goes through the model all at once, in one parallel pass. The answer comes out one token at a time, and every token needs its own pass through the whole model. Five times the price is a real gap in the work.\n\nCached input runs the other way. Send the same fifty-thousand-token prefix every turn, and the provider can keep what it computed and just read it back. That's about a tenth of the work, and some models now charge a twentieth. On Sonnet, ten cents a turn becomes half a cent. The catch: it only matches an exact prefix. Put a timestamp at the top, and one changed byte sends everything after it back to full price.",
